# Harnessing AI in Digital Humanities: Innovations and Implications

**Authors:** Julius Beneoluchi Odili

## Abstract
<jats:p>Digital Humanities (DH) is an interdisciplinary field that integrates humanities research with advanced digital tools and methods, while Artificial Intelligence (AI) involves computational systems capable of performing tasks traditionally requiring human intelligence, such as learning, reasoning, and language understanding. This paper explores the transformative role of AI—particularly machine learning, natural language processing (NLP), and data analysis—inenhancing core DH practices, including text mining, sentiment analysis, creative writing, and digital curation. The study finds that AI significantly improves the efficiency, accuracy, and scalability of DH research, facilitates interdisciplinary collaboration, and enables new insights through large-scale data processing and pattern recognition. However, challenges such as technical complexity, ethical concerns (e.g., bias and transparency), and potential dehumanization of scholarly practice persist. The discussion emphasizes the need for critical reflection and responsible implementation. The paper concludes by calling for global collaboration to develop standardized AI frameworks and recommends further research into expanding AI’s role in solving real-world problems through DH.</jats:p>

**Source:** https://doi.org/10.51846/jcsa.v2i1.3506

